---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="How the India AQI-Weather tracker is built, what the data means, and what it doesn't."
>
  <section class="container-prose section-pad">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-10">
      <div class="lg:col-span-7">
        <span class="badge badge-coral mb-3">About</span>
        <h1 class="display-xl" style="font-size:clamp(2.5rem, 1.8rem + 3.5vw, 4rem);">
          An honest picture of the air, district by district.
        </h1>
        <p class="text-body text-lg mt-6 max-w-2xl">
          The AQI.in dashboard exists because most air-quality tools
          either hide behind a paywall or only show the average across
          huge metro areas. We wanted the opposite: every district, the
          underlying number, the weather, and the history.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-5">
        <div class="card">
          <h2 class="display-sm" style="font-size:24px;">What's on this page</h2>
          <ul class="mt-3 flex flex-col gap-2 list-none p-0 text-body">
            <li><a href="#how" class="text-link">How the math works</a></li>
            <li><a href="#categories" class="text-link">The 6 AQI categories</a></li>
            <li><a href="#coverage" class="text-link">Coverage & limitations</a></li>
            <li><a href="#stack" class="text-link">The stack</a></li>
            <li><a href="#project" class="text-link">Project background</a></li>
            <li><a href="#contact" class="text-link">Get in touch</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="how" class="container-prose section-pad" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="badge badge-coral">01 · AQI math</span>
        <h2 class="display-sm mt-3" style="font-size:28px;">
          The National AQI series
        </h2>
        <p class="text-body mt-3">
          We follow India's National AQI (the CPCB series), which
          considers eight pollutants: PM2.5, PM10, NO₂, SO₂, CO, O₃, NH₃
          and Pb. Each pollutant has its own concentration-to-AQI
          piecewise-linear curve.
        </p>
        <p class="text-body mt-3">
          For every reading we compute a sub-index per pollutant and
          take the worst-case as the headline AQI. A reading is marked
          as "CPCB compliant" only when at least three pollutants —
          including one particulate — are present; otherwise the
          dashboard flags it as estimated.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="badge badge-coral">02 · Weather context</span>
        <h2 class="display-sm mt-3" style="font-size:28px;">
          Open-Meteo + IMD seasons
        </h2>
        <p class="text-body mt-3">
          Daily temperature, rainfall and wind come from the
          Open-Meteo historical and forecast APIs. We attach them to
          each station reading by district centroid.
        </p>
        <p class="text-body mt-3">
          Each day is then tagged with its IMD season — winter, summer,
          monsoon, post-monsoon — so the seasonal pattern on the
          dashboard reflects the calendar the meteorologists use.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="badge badge-coral">03 · Correlation</span>
        <h2 class="display-sm mt-3" style="font-size:28px;">
          Pearson r with weather
        </h2>
        <p class="text-body mt-3">
          For each district we compute Pearson's correlation coefficient
          between daily AQI and the daily mean of: wind speed
          (ventilation), and rainfall (washout).
        </p>
        <p class="text-body mt-3">
          A strong negative correlation with wind means pollution drops
          as wind picks up — usually true in flat, dry regions. A strong
          negative with rain means rainfall scrubs the air — usually
          strongest in coastal districts.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="badge badge-coral">04 · Honesty</span>
        <h2 class="display-sm mt-3" style="font-size:28px;">
          Coverage flags
        </h2>
        <p class="text-body mt-3">
          The same dashboard is honest about what it does and doesn't
          know. Districts with no recent CPCB reading show as
          <em>No data</em>, not as "AQI 0". A reading derived from fewer
          than three pollutants is shown as <em>Estimated</em>.
        </p>
        <p class="text-body mt-3">
          When the live API is unreachable, the site falls back to a
          bundled sample dataset so every page still renders. A banner
          at the top of the dashboard tells you which one you're seeing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="categories" class="container-prose section-pad" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="card">
      <h2 class="display-md" style="font-size:28px;">The 6 AQI categories</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm" style="border-collapse: collapse;">
          <thead>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);">
              <th class="py-3 pr-4" style="font-weight:500; color:var(--color-muted);">AQI</th>
              <th class="py-3 pr-4" style="font-weight:500; color:var(--color-muted);">Category</th>
              <th class="py-3 pr-4" style="font-weight:500; color:var(--color-muted);">Health</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              [
                {
                  range: "0 – 50",
                  cat: "Good",
                  color: "#2a7a2a",
                  bg: "#dff3df",
                  health: "Air quality is satisfactory; air pollution poses little or no risk.",
                },
                {
                  range: "51 – 100",
                  cat: "Satisfactory",
                  color: "#486b1a",
                  bg: "#ecf5cf",
                  health: "Acceptable; some pollutants may be a concern for very sensitive people.",
                },
                {
                  range: "101 – 200",
                  cat: "Moderate",
                  color: "#7a5a00",
                  bg: "#fff3bf",
                  health: "Members of sensitive groups may experience health effects.",
                },
                {
                  range: "201 – 300",
                  cat: "Poor",
                  color: "#8a4a00",
                  bg: "#ffe2bf",
                  health: "Everyone may begin to experience health effects; sensitive groups more serious.",
                },
                {
                  range: "301 – 400",
                  cat: "Very Poor",
                  color: "#ffffff",
                  bg: "#c2452f",
                  health: "Health warnings of emergency conditions; the entire population is affected.",
                },
                {
                  range: "401 – 500",
                  cat: "Severe",
                  color: "#ffffff",
                  bg: "#6e1b1b",
                  health: "Health alert: everyone may experience more serious health effects.",
                },
              ].map((r) => (
                <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline-soft);">
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 display-sm" style="font-size:18px;">
                    {r.range}
                  </td>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                    <span
                      class="badge"
                      style={`color:${r.color}; background:${r.bg}; text-transform:uppercase;`}
                    >
                      {r.cat}
                    </span>
                  </td>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-body">{r.health}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="coverage" class="container-prose section-pad" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="card-coral">
      <h2 class="display-md" style="color:#fff;">Coverage & limitations</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mt-4" style="color:#fff;">
        <div>
          <h3 class="display-sm" style="color:#fff; font-size:20px;">
            What the dashboard covers
          </h3>
          <ul class="mt-3 list-disc pl-5" style="line-height:1.7;">
            <li>All LGD-coded districts in India (≈ 200 in seed).</li>
            <li>Active CPCB monitoring stations, refreshed daily.</li>
            <li>Daily temperature, rainfall and wind from Open-Meteo.</li>
            <li>Seasonal averages derived from the available history.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="display-sm" style="color:#fff; font-size:20px;">
            What's out of scope
          </h3>
          <ul class="mt-3 list-disc pl-5" style="line-height:1.7;">
            <li>Hourly forecasts (the dashboard is daily).</li>
            <li>Pollutant-level deep-dive (per-station PM2.5, etc.).</li>
            <li>Health advisories at sub-district resolution.</li>
            <li>Real-time push (the data is end-of-day for the day before).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="stack" class="container-prose section-pad" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <div class="card-canvas">
        <h2 class="display-sm" style="font-size:24px;">Backend</h2>
        <p class="text-body mt-3">
          FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, and SQLite (Postgres in production with
          TimescaleDB). The AQI math uses the official CPCB breakpoint
          tables and computes a per-pollutant sub-index.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc pl-5 text-body" style="line-height:1.7;">
          <li>FastAPI for the JSON API.</li>
          <li>SQLAlchemy ORM + Pydantic schemas for contracts.</li>
          <li>pytest suite for the calculator, rollups and correlation.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card-canvas">
        <h2 class="display-sm" style="font-size:24px;">Frontend</h2>
        <p class="text-body mt-3">
          Astro 7 with Tailwind CSS v4. The site is fully static, with
          one small client-side island on the dashboard that listens
          for district and range changes.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc pl-5 text-body" style="line-height:1.7;">
          <li>Astro for routing and zero-JS static rendering.</li>
          <li>Tailwind v4 via the Vite plugin.</li>
          <li>Inline SVG for the map, gauge and charts.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="project" class="container-prose section-pad" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="card">
      <h2 class="display-sm" style="font-size:24px;">Project background</h2>
      <p class="text-body mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        AQI.in started as a backend blueprint for an LGD-coded,
        district-level AQI and weather tracker. The original code went
        through several bug-fix passes: a copy-paste error that had
        NO₂ and SO₂ sharing the same breakpoint table, a wind-speed
        column that didn't exist, an O₃ band that didn't match the
        official CPCB spec. The frontend you see here was missing
        from earlier rounds and is now part of the project.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        The dashboard is intentionally a static site so it can be
        deployed anywhere, including behind the FastAPI app itself if
        desired. When the live API is reachable, the dashboard shows
        live data; when it isn't, the same site falls back to a
        deterministic sample dataset so every page still works
        end-to-end.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="contact" class="container-prose section-pad" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="card-coral text-center" style="text-align:center;">
      <h2 class="display-md" style="color:#fff;">Have a dataset to share?</h2>
      <p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.9);" class="mt-3 max-w-xl mx-auto">
        We're always looking for more open datasets — more pollutants,
        more stations, longer history. Drop a note.
      </p>
      <a
        href="mailto:hello@aqi.in"
        class="btn btn-coral-inverse mt-6 inline-flex"
      >
        hello@aqi.in
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
